<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf</h1><p class="st-meta">1678 CE – 1848 CE · capital Ebersdorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tiny Reuss lordship in Thuringia that became a stronghold of Pietism and the Moravian Brethren.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ebersdorf was separated from Reuss-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lobenstein">Lobenstein</a> in 1678 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-x-reuss-of-ebersdorf">Heinrich X</a>, a younger son of the Lobenstein line, creating a small county within the Younger Line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-highlands">Thuringian Highlands</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts of Ebersdorf were devout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietists</a> who turned their small residence into a religious centre. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</a>, founder of the renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdmuthe-dorothea-von-zinzendorf">Erdmuthe Dorothea of Reuss-Ebersdorf</a> in 1722, and a Moravian congregation settled in Ebersdorf. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria's</a> maternal grandmother came from the family. The rulers became princes in 1806.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ebersdorf inherited Reuss-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lobenstein">Lobenstein</a> in 1824. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-lxxii-prince-reuss-of-lobenstein-and-ebersdorf">Heinrich LXXII</a>, an eccentric absolutist, abdicated during the revolution of 1848, and his lands passed to Reuss-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a>, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-reuss-younger-line">Principality of Reuss Younger Line</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1678 CE – 1848 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.09%;width:3.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1678 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ebersdorf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heinrich X, Heinrich LXXII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Pietism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reuß-Ebersdorf, Reuss-Lobenstein-Ebersdorf</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1763&amp;focus=principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf" aria-label="Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4975 413 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5295 669L5265 680M5295 669L5275 669"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5265 680h0M5275 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5295 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5295 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ebersdorf. <a href="/atlas?year=1763&amp;focus=principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
